research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md-142-| Justin Kaidi, *Introduction to Generalized Symmetries*, arXiv:2603.08798v3 (2026-09-18). INSPIRE record 3128125 had no DOI/journal record in the 2026-10-23 query. | Retrieved 106-page PDF. Inspected the abstract/introduction (PDF pp. 1–5), anomaly/inflow and Maxwell mixed-anomaly discussion (pp. 17–20), higher-group example (pp. 30–32), finite fusion-category excerpts (pp. 36–39), categorical gauging and SymTFT excerpts (pp. 52–55 and 58–60), and selected 3+1D applications (pp. 70–74). The author describes the notes as an introduction/review; the 3+1D section explicitly says the space of noninvertible symmetries is less understood and offers model vignettes rather than a comprehensive survey. No uninspected proof is used here. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md-143-| D. Gaiotto, A. Kapustin, N. Seiberg, B. Willett, *Generalized Global Symmetries*, arXiv:1412.5148v2, JHEP 02 (2015) 172. | Retrieved the 77-page PDF and read PDF pp. 1–8: abstract/introduction, precise codimension/charge definition, defects, background coupling/gauging setup. The later anomaly and example sections were not read for this note. Use it as the foundational higher-form source, not as a claim that its 2015 scope covers current categorical/higher-dimensional developments. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:144:| F. Benini, C. Córdova, P.-S. Hsin, *On 2-Group Global Symmetries and Their Anomalies*, arXiv:1803.09336v3, JHEP 02 (2019) 121. | Retrieved the 83-page PDF and inspected PDF pp. 1–13: abstract/contents and introduction through the definitions of \((G,A,\rho,\beta)\), 2-group backgrounds, the distinction between 2-group Ward identities and c-number anomalies, and the anomaly-inflow setup. Later dimension-by-dimension classifications and proofs were not read here; the local mathematics needed for any imported case must be checked independently. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md-145-| D. S. Freed, M. J. Hopkins, *Reflection Positivity and Invertible Topological Phases*, arXiv:1604.06527v6. | Retrieved 141-page PDF and read PDF pp. 1–6: abstract, introduction, Theorem 1.1, and its explicit assumptions/caveat. Their result concerns deformation classes of specified reflection-positive invertible extended topological field theories via a Thom spectrum/Anderson dual. The lattice-to-low-energy-QFT step in the introduction is heuristic, and this is not a theorem that any given QFT anomaly is classified by an unqualified group \(\Omega^{?}\). The paper’s proof and detailed spin/group computations were not read for this note. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md-146-| Adel Bilal, *Lectures on Anomalies*, arXiv:0802.0634. | Retrieved 108-page PDF; inspected the overview/contents and selected PDF pp. 75, 78, 80–81, 90–91, and 94–95 for local BRST cohomology, descent, anomaly-polynomial and mixed gauge-gravitational formulas. This supports the perturbative/local strand only. It is not a global-anomaly or generalized-category classification, and no formula is imported without convention-specific review. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md-147-| Diego Delmastro, Adar Sharon, Yunqin Zheng, *Non-Local Conserved Currents and Continuous Non-Invertible Symmetries*, arXiv:2507.22976v2 (2025-08-15), JHEP 11 (2025) 072 (INSPIRE record 2955822, publication record present). | Retrieved 55-page PDF; read abstract/introduction PDF pp. 1–3 and conclusion/future directions p. 42. The paper’s proven construction is focused on \(1+1\)-dimensional CFTs. Its own open questions include extension to general QFT/higher dimensions, classification of deformations preserving the supporting topological line, the relation of nonlocal currents to quantum-group charges, and background fields for continuous noninvertible symmetries. It says the converse/current characterization is not yet fully understood; this is an explicit frontier lead, not a theorem for arbitrary dimensions. |
